new york times best-selling author  jacqueline woodson has written more than  30 books for young readers for memoir  brown girl dreaming won the national  book award each of her books places  black teenagers and children  at the center of their own story  woodson's new book is called  before the ever after look at that cover  i love that cupboard it'll mean  something  i think and i love the title too it  tells the story of zj  who struggles to understand his dad's  memory loss while trying to hold on to  the good memories  of the past woodson also won this year's  hans christian anderson award that's the  highest international honor  for children's book authors and she's  with us now congratulations to you on  that jaclyn woodson always good to see  you  so let's start with great to see you i  know i really appreciate you getting up  so let's talk about before the ever  after because it focuses on  um zj i keep wanting to say jay-z but zj  his dad is a uh was a former  professional football player  he starts noticing that his dad is  having memory loss his personalities  changing  and it's terrifying and baffling to him  at the same time  zj's about 12 13. why did you pick this  i could see where you were going once it  started i went oh i know  what's happening here why did you pick  this this particular topic  i think the first and foremost reason is  because i  wanted to tell a story about a  father-son relationship a very  deep and loving and cherished father-son  relationship and what  happens to it and also i wanted to talk  about  the damaging effects of contact sports  and  and how it's not just about losing these  kind of  anonymous bodies that it impacts real  people  and real lives real families and the  effect of mental illness in the family  too sometimes and that's often a very  difficult conversation  to have for anybody it is so  true and i think especially young people  one great thing  that books do is they give young people  this language by which they could talk  about stuff they might not have felt  safe talking about or might not have had  the vocabulary to talk about before and  so  with before the ever after i was hoping  to do some of that to give young people  the language and  the permission to begin to have these  conversations  the other thing that really touched me  was zj and his friends you call him the  fantastic four there's derry and daniel  um another name starts with the oh and  ollie and ali  and that his friends are there for him  and i think it's so unusual that you  give boys little boys the opportunity to  express their feelings the way these two  these four do number one they eat very  well peanut butter and banana sandwiches  i love those on soft bread  and then they have a thing with grilled  cheese and the cheese is streamed from  the bread as they're eating it  and they're having really deep personal  conversations  yet really bonding together i think  that's an important message to  send were you doing that you're mother  of a son were you doing that  deliberately  yeah i think one thing is a writer you  know what you're doing and you don't  know what you're doing so it's the  conscious the unconscious the  subconscious  and with the 11 12 year old boys i mean  they do have these kind of conversations  we don't always hear them  we don't always believe that boys are  actually having these conversations but  they  are and and i really wanted to put on  the page  these young boys who actually really  talk to each other and not only talk but  actively  listen to each other and um and  and support each other i think we think  of that kind of thing the active  listening the support the  having these deep conversations is  something that  older people do our older kids do but  young people start doing it  from an early age and continue to and i  really wanted to give  zj his boys his support system yeah that  was that's  they have very very sweet moments i also  i'm fascinated by you 